#{[#edb/ref 17592186050771 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612} :activity/id #uuid "b16543e4-9398-58b9-b3c3-d37c4e46b486" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070014 :tutorial/content "How can we solve an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \\,?\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u=x^2+1$, then the derivative is $u' = 2x,$ and both $u$ and $u'$ are present in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'}(\\overbrace{x^2+1}^{u})^3\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution. So, let's proceed. If we let ${\\color{red}{u=x^2+1}},$ then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\color{blue}\\textrm{d}u=2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now see that the change of variables from $x$ to $u$ is convenient because if we substitute, we get\n\n$$\n\\int {\\color{blue}{2x}}({\\color{red}{x^2+1}})^3\\,{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}x}} =  \\int \\color{red}(x^2+1)^{\\color{black}3}\\, \\color{blue}{2x\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\color{black}= \\int {\\color{red}u}^{\\color{black}3}\\, {\\color{blue}{\\,\\textrm{d}u}} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the integral on the right since it's the integral of a power function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last step, we change the variable back to $x$ by replacing $u=x^2+1.$ So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \n=\\dfrac{(x^2+1)^4}{4}+C.\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{4}}{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[({x}^{2}+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068564 :question/id #uuid "e2579380-1933-5819-a39e-9ffcfdafeeee" :question/math-academy-id "e-452" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}(2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2x^3+3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068565 :question/id #uuid "9bf7d343-8d3d-5194-93c4-7b80bc1e3609" :question/math-academy-id "e-453" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x\\left(1+2x^2\\right)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "The $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(1+2x^2).$ The derivative of $(1+2x^2)$ is $4x,$ so we're out by a constant factor of $4$. However, since we're out only by a constant factor, substitution will still work!\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+2x^2.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{u}^{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070015 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive." :tutorial/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068566 :question/id #uuid "49f679c4-bbc4-59a5-9b0c-0f3b69d7cf57" :question/math-academy-id "e-454" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068628 :question/id #uuid "0461f8ba-c3c5-5e22-a679-32ee4f2a01b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6149" :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068971}] [#edb/ref 17592186050163 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101854} :activity/id #uuid "8d861dcd-02c5-53fa-858a-2be900cad05a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101952 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe denote the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}3$rd row and $\\color{red}2$nd column using the notation $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}.$\n\nThe first subscript represents the row and the second subscript represents the column. So $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}=8,$ as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{8} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can denote every entry of a matrix in this way.\nFor instance, $a_{{\\color{blue}4}\\color{red}1}$ denotes the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}4$th row and $\\color{red}1$st column.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33} \\\\ {a}_{41} & {a}_{42} & {a}_{43}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100683 :question/id #uuid "622baf41-fe35-55ee-bfc6-f1e6a2cb089a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4371" :question/problem "Find the entry $a_{23}$ of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $a_{23}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $3$rd column. Therefore, $a_{23}={\\color{blue}{-3}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100989 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100684 :question/id #uuid "fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4372" :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix ${B},$ where \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Square Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101953 :tutorial/content "In general, we denote the entry of a matrix $A$ that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}i$th row and $\\color{red}j$th column by $a_{{\\color{blue}i}\\color{red}j}.$\n\nIf $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & \\cdots & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & \\cdots & {a}_{2n} \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & \\cdots & {a}_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere each entry $a_{ij}$ is defined for $i = 1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j =1,2,\\ldots,n.$" :tutorial/title "General Form of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100685 :question/id #uuid "432a6a1a-ba9d-5aa0-b758-d4d32e9992f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4373" :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $C$ given below. Describe it in terms of $c_{ij}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & \\boxed{5} \\\\ 34 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry lies at the intersection of the $1$st row and $3$rd column. Therefore, we have $c_{13}={\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}}] :activity/title "Index Notation for Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101034}] [#edb/ref 17592186050473 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176635} :activity/id #uuid "a22648a9-1107-5fad-945a-32867ab1ed64" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177095 :tutorial/content "To find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to a given line $y=mx+b,$ we can just take the **negative reciprocal** of the given slope $m.$ So, the slope of the perpendicular line would be $-\\dfrac{1}{m}.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $y = 3x + 1,$ which has a slope of $m=3.$ The negative reciprocal of $3$ is $-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ So, any line with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ will be perpendicular to the given line, because\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, some lines with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ are plotted below. We can see that they are all perpendicular to the given line $y=3x+1.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2772aa0cbe3e736e1129b52c59d3a7c36759a0ccf7b7a6b409cce09b2b22497.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :question/id #uuid "ca9ba6cf-860f-5868-b91f-1dff84406f22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14357" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(-8,7).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $l_1$ denote the line $y = 4x - 1,$ and let $l_2$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of $l_2$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of $l_1.$ Since $l_1$ has a slope of $4,$ the line $l_2$ must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-8,7).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\ny - y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-(-8)) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x+8)\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x - 2 \\[5pt]\ny &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x + 5\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175737 :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175741 :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-3x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1) = (6,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-6) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}}] :activity/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176113}] [#edb/ref 17592186048245 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185245} :activity/id #uuid "aa1811b2-1877-5406-b70c-b443bbb27d74" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185377 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x e^{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then both $u$ and its derivative $u'=2x$ appear in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} e^{\\overbrace{x^2}^u}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nLet $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute our expressions into the integral and evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,{{2x\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{d}u} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185246}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184349 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183945 :question/id #uuid "3e9586ee-12d1-5dbb-81f6-0f0a4998e00a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14402" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u = x^3+1.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot \\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184350 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183946 :question/id #uuid "ab4772a2-f980-5ad7-a377-34e34b95be83" :question/math-academy-id "e-14403" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u= 1-x^4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = -4x^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int {x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} }\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=\\int {{3^{1-x^4}}\\cdot x^3 }\\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n&=  \\int {3^{u}} \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\int {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Other Exponential Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184351 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183947 :question/id #uuid "70fd3433-e905-5643-8e55-0bdf774d781e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14404" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$"} :knowledge-point/title "More Complex Cases"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185378 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{3} xe^{x^{2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step1: ** Change of variable in the integrand.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = 2x \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u = x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe create a table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $9$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{9}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{9}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Often, there are several ways in which we can evaluate an integral using a change of variable. Whatever we use depends on the particular form of the integrand. In this tutorial, we take care of the applications of the properties\n\\[ \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) = e^x \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(a^x\\right) = a^x\\ln a\\]\nwhich generally leads to nice integrands after the change of variable." :tutorial/title "Definite Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184352 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183948 :question/id #uuid "a99e8d2c-1934-57a9-9a6a-578ec9230f6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14407" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt {\\ln 4}} 16xe^{-2x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u=-2x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-4x\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n-4 \\, \\textrm{d}u=16x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt {\\ln 4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\ln 4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}16x{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot 16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-4({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{15}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184369}]}
